  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13946716/assets/9e8cc0891defe34b22259c4a7b84a467a8fd13474b2518c255d630c346985526.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The function $g$ is a part of the graph of the function $y=2\\sqrt{x}-1,$ as shown in the figure above. Find the number of weeks $x$ at which the stock price given by this function was $$3,000.$\n\n*Hint: Remember that $g$ is measured in thousands of dollars.*\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $x\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "We have to solve $g(x)=3,$ or equivalently, $2\\sqrt{x}-1=3.$\n\nFirst, we isolate the term $2\\sqrt{x}$ by adding $1$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{x}-1 & =3 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{x} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we isolate the square root by multiplying by $\\frac{1}{2}$ on both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{x} & =4 \\\\ \\sqrt{x} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the square root is isolated, we can proceed as usual. Squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x} & =2 \\\\ (\\sqrt{x})^{2} & ={2}^{2} \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the result by plugging it into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{4}-1 & \\overset{?}{=}3 \\\\ 2\\cdot 2-1 & \\overset{?}{=}3 \\\\ 4-1 & =3\\,\\,\u2713\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe value $x=4$ satisfies the original equation, so we can accept it as a solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution to $2\\sqrt{x}-1=3$  is $x=4.$",
